@1185[end]. If (these things) are not ends, but means, they are blessings.

@1187. As the end which is the love... through the understanding provides for itself means, through which it comes forth - the last end, to which the first end progresses through the means, is the end coming forth -existens-, and this is the use, - it follows that the end loves the means when they perform this use; and that it does not love them if they do not perform it; and that then it rejects them, and through the understanding provides for itself others...

@--[2]. For example: a priest whose principal end is the love of money... His means are the ministry, the Word, doctrine, learning, preaching from these, and through these the instruction of the men of the Church, and their reformation and salvation. These means are esteemed by him from the end and for the sake of the end; but still they are not loved... for it is the opulence which is loved, because this is the first and last end, and the former end is the all in the means. (Other examples.)

